and `expected` now include notices in the session’s own transcript and state that they never finalize that session — closes LLR-347’s own-notice exception.\n- TC-389 `verifies`: adds `LLR-347`, `LLR-351`, `LLR-355`, and `LLR-356`; `method` adds open messages, malformed headers, spool/root/worktree logs, and duplicate names; `expected` replaces `\"ordered contribution key\"` with the actual immutable key plus ordered contribution-name contract, and covers cursor/range, unchanged, refusal, own-contribution, outcome, successful-exit, and later-final-range arms.\n- TC-390 `method` adds an unreadable governing record; `expected` adds skipped output and refusal of every governed transcript — closes LLR-351 unreadable-record and LLR-355 skipped-outcome arms.\n- TC-391 `method` adds missing shapes and nonpositive nesting; `expected` adds continued recording of other transcripts, positive non-boolean nesting, and refusal exit 1.\n- TC-392 `method` adds contribution-body inspection; `expected` now requires the fixed counts/identities/timestamps summary with neither transcript text nor raw usage.\n- TC-394 `method` adds configured/default homes, explicit/ambient selection, subagents with an absent main, and commit refusal; `tier`: `\"Smoke\"` → `\"Full\"`; `expected` adds home/session precedence, subagent handling, and refusal-exit-one behavior.\n- TC-395 `method` and `expected` now require ingest before handoff and the handoff’s own session identity.\n\nGap disposition:\n\n- All section 1a arms are now stated in a TC that cites the pertinent LLR, with reciprocal `test_refs` added for the prior “by substance” cases.\n- The LLR-349 wording issue is closed by distinguishing its immutable contribution key from the distinct, ordered contribution names/stamps; the code orders the latter, not the key.\n- TC-393 remains unchanged: SR-244 itself states the no-gate-authority obligation it verifies.\n- The “also noted” root-record-directory/de-duplication, nonpositive-depth/missing-pair